# Breadcrumb

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A breadcrumb trail lists the parents of the current page, from the top down, so people can see where they are and go up a level. It suits a deep hierarchy, such as a device inside a room inside a home.

Home Assistant has no breadcrumb element. Dashboards do not show one, and the closest native feature is the back arrow in a page header. This page describes a Civica pattern that you build yourself, for example in a Markdown card or a custom card.

This page covers:

- Breadcrumb
  - Civica Breadcrumb Pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

1. A navigation area with the label “Breadcrumb”.
2. An ordered list, one item for each parent, from the top level down.
3. A link in each item, pointing to a real parent page.
4. The current page, either left out or shown as plain text marked as the current page.
5. Separators between items that are drawn for the eye only, so they are not read out as part of a link name.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-breadcrumb-pattern"><code>Civica Breadcrumb Pattern</code></h3>

There are two ways to treat the current page:

- **Left out.** The page heading names the current page, so the trail lists only its parents. This is the simpler choice.
- **Shown as text.** The last item is plain text marked with `aria-current="page"`. It is never a link, because a link to the page you are already on does nothing useful.

Both ways use real parent destinations, wrap onto a new line when there is not enough width, and keep separators out of the names of the links.

Theme variables supply only the link colour and the text style: `link-color`, `primary-text-color`, `secondary-text-color` and the `ha-font-*` keys. There is no Home Assistant element to style, so card-mod has nothing to attach to. Build the trail from ordinary Markdown links:

```yaml
type: markdown
content: |
  [Home](/lovelace/home) / [Rooms](/lovelace/rooms) / Kitchen
```

This simple form is a starting point. It does not give you a labelled navigation area or an ordered list, which need your own markup or a custom card.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A trail shows the full route up in one place, so people do not need to press back several times.
- It works with the page heading: the heading says where you are, the trail says what is above it.
- It takes a little room at the top of a page and wraps onto several lines for long names.
- Home Assistant does not draw it for you, and a theme alone cannot add it.
- It shows hierarchy, not a history of dashboards people have visited.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a breadcrumb trail only where a hierarchy is deep enough to need it, such as a device detail page. Home Assistant’s sidebar already tells people which dashboard they are on, so do not add a trail to a top-level view.

Do not use it for a step-by-step journey, and do not put it next to a back link. For a journey, use a [Back link](back-link.html).

Use real parent routes. Do not invent a hierarchy from entity identifiers or names. Keep labels plain and the same as the headings of the pages they open. Keep to a few levels and put the trail in the same place on every page.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: use a labelled navigation area and an ordered list.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context): each link name makes sense on its own.
- 1.4.10 Reflow and 1.4.4 Resize Text: the trail wraps and nothing is cut off.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: do not rely on colour alone to show that an item is a link.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured.
- 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation: put the trail in the same place on each page.
- 2.4.8 Location (AAA) is helped by a trail.

Check long names, 320 px width and larger text in light and dark modes. Measure contrast, link size and screen reader output in your own setup. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

